---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site, links, sameAs } from '../data/site';
import authorPhoto from '../assets/author.jpg';

const books = (await getCollection('books')).filter((b) => !b.data.draft);

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'ProfilePage',
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: site.name,
    url: site.url,
    image: new URL('/og.jpg', site.url).href,
    jobTitle: 'Author and Japanese interpreter',
    description: site.description,
    sameAs,
    knowsLanguage: ['en', 'ja', 'ko', 'es'],
    worksFor: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'YesJapan Corporation' },
  },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="George Trombley spent eighteen years as a professional Japanese interpreter before writing the Japanese From Zero! series. A short account of how that happened."
  type="profile"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="section wrap">
    <header class="about__head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">About</p>
        <h1 class="about__title">George Trombley</h1>
        <p class="about__standfirst">
          Professional Japanese interpreter for eighteen years. Author of {books.length}
          language books. As of this October, a novelist too.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="about__figure">
        <Image
          src={authorPhoto}
          alt="George Trombley"
          width={640}
          widths={[280, 420, 640]}
          sizes="(max-width: 48rem) 50vw, 18rem"
          loading="eager"
          class="about__photo"
        />
      </figure>
    </header>

    <div class="about__columns">
      <!--
        The prose measure is deliberately short (~34rem) for readability, which
        at desktop widths left more than half the page empty. The aside fills it
        with the facts a reader would otherwise have to mine out of the prose,
        and sticks while the article scrolls past.
      -->
      <aside class="about__aside" aria-label="At a glance">
        <div class="about__aside-inner">
          <p class="label">At a glance</p>
          <dl class="about__glance">
            <div>
              <dt>Interpreting</dt>
              <dd>18 years, Japanese &harr; English</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Clients</dt>
              <dd>Microsoft, IBM, NTT DoCoMo, Lucent Technologies</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Books</dt>
              <dd>{books.length} across Japanese, Korean, Kanji and Spanish</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Publishers</dt>
              <dd>YesJapan Corporation &middot; Sandspeck Press</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Fiction</dt>
              <dd><a href="/fiction">Fate Squared</a>, 29 October 2026</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </aside>

    <div class="prose about__body">
      <p>
        I did not set out to write textbooks. I set out to interpret, and for
        eighteen years that is what I did — simultaneous and consecutive Japanese
        interpretation for Microsoft, IBM, NTT DoCoMo and Lucent Technologies,
        in boardrooms and factories and conference halls across North America,
        Europe, Asia and the Middle East.
      </p>

      <p>
        Interpreting is a strange education. You are not just carrying words
        across; you are carrying intent, register, hesitation, and the thing the
        speaker meant but did not say. Do it for long enough and you develop a
        very specific sense of which parts of a language actually carry meaning
        and which parts a beginner can safely be spared for another six months.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the books start at zero</h2>

      <p>
        Every Japanese textbook I picked up made the same demand: memorise
        forty-six hiragana characters, then forty-six katakana, and then we will
        begin. It is a reasonable way to organise a writing system and a terrible
        way to teach a person. Nobody learns to speak by first learning to spell.
      </p>

      <p>
        So <em>Japanese From Zero!</em> introduces the writing system a few
        characters at a time, immediately using each one in sentences built from
        words the reader already knows. By the end of Book 1 you are reading
        hiragana without noticing that you learned it. By Book 3 the romaji is
        gone entirely. It is not a trick — it is just sequencing, which is most
        of what teaching is.
      </p>

      <p>
        The series was written with <strong>Yukari Takenaka</strong> and refined
        over fifteen years of classroom teaching, which is the part that matters.
        Every explanation in those books survived contact with real students who
        did not understand the first three versions of it.
      </p>

      <h2>Beyond Japanese</h2>

      <p>
        The method turned out to travel. <em>Korean From Zero!</em> was written
        with the Korean linguist <strong>Reed Bullen</strong> and a team of
        native-speaking co-authors — Jiyoon Kim, Sunhee Bong, Sieun An and
        Myunghee Ham. <em>Kanji From Zero!</em> applied the same sequencing logic
        to the characters. And <em>Spanish From Zero!</em>, written with{' '}
        <strong>Hugo Canedo</strong>, took it into a fourth language.
      </p>

      <p>
        Alongside the books there is <a href={links.fromZero}>From Zero!</a>, the
        online course built on the same material, and a long-running video series
        that has been going about as long as the books have.
      </p>

      <h2>And now, fiction</h2>

      <p>
        <a href={links.fateSquared}><em>Fate Squared</em></a> is my first novel,
        out on 29 October 2026 from Sandspeck Press. It is a thriller, it has
        nothing to do with language learning, and writing it was the hardest and
        most enjoyable thing I have done in years.
      </p>

      <p>
        I also write <a href={links.substack}>essays</a> — short, irregular, and
        mostly about the things I notice rather than the things I know.
      </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="about__links">
      <hr class="rule" />
      <p class="label">Elsewhere</p>
      <ul role="list">
        <li><a href={links.fromZero}>fromzero.com</a> — the courses</li>
        <li><a href={links.fateSquared}>fatesquared.com</a> — the novel</li>
        <li><a href={links.substack}>Substack</a> — essays</li>
        <li><a href={links.amazonAuthor}>Amazon author page</a></li>
        <li><a href={links.goodreads}>Goodreads</a></li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about__head {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 18rem);
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    align-items: center;
    margin-block-end: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .about__title {
    font-size: var(--text-4xl);
    margin-block: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-md);
  }

  .about__standfirst {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    max-inline-size: 30ch;
  }

  .about__figure {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0;
    justify-self: end;
    inline-size: 100%;
  }

  .about__figure::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: var(--space-sm) calc(var(--space-sm) * -1) calc(var(--space-sm) * -1)
      var(--space-sm);
    border: 1px solid var(--seal);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    z-index: -1;
  }

  .about__photo {
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    filter: grayscale(1) contrast(1.04);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-cover);
  }

  /* Prose left, sticky facts right. The aside comes first in the DOM so it is
     reachable early by keyboard and screen reader, and is moved to the right
     column visually — the two are independent, so nothing is re-ordered
     illogically for assistive tech. */
  .about__columns {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--measure)) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-2xl);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .about__body {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1;
  }

  .about__aside {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1;
  }

  .about__aside-inner {
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(var(--square) * 2.5);
    padding: var(--space-md);
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  }

  .about__glance {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .about__glance dt {
    font-size: var(--text-2xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }

  .about__glance dd {
    margin: 0.25em 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .about__glance a {
    text-decoration-color: var(--seal);
  }

  .about__links {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .about__links ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5em;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }

  .about__links a {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration-color: var(--seal);
  }

  /* Below this width the two columns would both be too narrow to read, so the
     aside drops under the prose and stops sticking. */
  @media (max-width: 62rem) {
    .about__columns {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-xl);
    }

    .about__body,
    .about__aside {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: auto;
    }

    /* Prose first on narrow screens; the facts are a supplement there. */
    .about__body {
      order: 1;
    }

    .about__aside {
      order: 2;
    }

    .about__aside-inner {
      position: static;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .about__head {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-lg);
    }

    .about__figure {
      order: -1;
      justify-self: start;
      max-inline-size: 13rem;
    }
  }
</style>
